</video> </p></div>";}i:12;O:8:"stdClass":18:{s:2:"id";s:1:"5";s:2:"cm";s:2:"12";s:3:"mod";s:5:"label";s:7:"section";s:1:"2";s:13:"sectionnumber";s:1:"2";s:9:"sectionid";s:2:"13";s:6:"module";s:2:"12";s:5:"added";s:10:"1753460782";s:7:"visible";s:1:"1";s:19:"visibleoncoursepage";i:1;s:10:"visibleold";s:1:"1";s:10:"completion";s:1:"1";s:19:"completionpassgrade";s:1:"0";s:15:"showdescription";s:1:"1";s:15:"downloadcontent";s:1:"1";s:4:"lang";s:0:"";s:4:"name";s:62:"video3: Selection of Programming Language for Embedded Systems";s:7:"content";s:503:"<div class="no-overflow"><h3 data-start="149" data-end="223"><strong data-start="156" data-end="223">Selection of Programming Language for Embedded Systems<br><br> <video title="3. Selction of correct programming language for embedded System" controls="controls" width="1280" height="721">
        <source src="https://lms.circuitmakers.in/pluginfile.php/40/mod_label/intro/3.%20Selction%20of%20correct%20programming%20language%20for%20embedded%20System.mp4">
    
</video> <br></strong></h3></div>";}i:13;O:8:"stdClass":18:{s:2:"id";s:1:"6";s:2:"cm";s:2:"13";s:3:"mod";s:5:"label";s:7:"section";s:1:"2";s:13:"sectionnumber";s:1:"2";s:9:"sectionid";s:2:"13";s:6:"module";s:2:"12";s:5:"added";s:10:"1753461040";s:7:"visible";s:1:"1";s:19:"visibleoncoursepage";i:1;s:10:"visibleold";s:1:"1";s:10:"completion";s:1:"1";s:19:"completionpassgrade";s:1:"0";s:15:"showdescription";s:1:"1";s:15:"downloadcontent";s:1:"1";s:4:"lang";s:0:"";s:4:"name";s:43:"video4: Difference between C and Embedded C";s:7:"content";s:1741:"<div class="no-overflow"><p> </p>
<h3 data-start="138" data-end="184"><strong data-start="145" data-end="184">Difference Between C and Embedded C</strong></h3>
<p data-start="186" data-end="346">While <strong data-start="192" data-end="197">C</strong> and <strong data-start="202" data-end="216">Embedded C</strong> share the same syntax and core language structure, they differ in their <strong data-start="289" data-end="345">application domain and level of hardware interaction</strong>.</p>
<div class="_tableContainer_80l1q_1">
<div class="_tableWrapper_80l1q_14 group flex w-fit flex-col-reverse" tabindex="-1">
<div class="sticky end-(--thread-content-margin) h-0 self-end select-none">
<div class="absolute end-0 flex items-end"> </div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<hr data-start="1391" data-end="1394">
<h3 data-start="1396" data-end="1410">✅ Summary:</h3>
<ul data-start="1411" data-end="1633">
<li data-start="1411" data-end="1479">
<p data-start="1413" data-end="1479"><strong data-start="1413" data-end="1418">C</strong> is versatile, used for a wide range of software development.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1480" data-end="1633">
<p data-start="1482" data-end="1633"><strong data-start="1482" data-end="1496">Embedded C</strong> is an adaptation of C tailored for <strong data-start="1532" data-end="1565">microcontroller-based systems</strong> with low-level hardware interaction and tight resource constraints.<br><br></p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><video title="4. Difference between C and Embedded C" controls="controls" width="1280" height="721">
        <source src="https://lms.circuitmakers.in/pluginfile.php/41/mod_label/intro/4.%20Difference%20between%20C%20and%20Embedded%20C.mp4">
    
</video> </p></div>";}i:15;O:8:"stdClass":15:{s:2:"id";s:1:"3";s:2:"cm";s:2:"15";s:3:"mod";s:4:"quiz";s:7:"section";s:1:"2";s:13:"sectionnumber";s:1:"2";s:9:"sectionid";s:2:"13";s:6:"module";s:2:"17";s:5:"added";s:10:"1753461942";s:7:"visible";s:1:"1";s:19:"visibleoncoursepage";i:1;s:10:"visibleold";s:1:"1";s:19:"completionpassgrade";s:1:"0";s:15:"downloadcontent";s:1:"1";s:4:"lang";s:0:"";s:4:"name";s:6:"Quiz 2";}}s:12:"sectioncache";a:3:{i:11;O:8:"stdClass":7:{s:2:"id";s:2:"11";s:7:"section";s:1:"0";s:4:"name";s:29:"What is an Embedded systems ?";s:7:"summary";s:2745:"<p data-start="39" data-end="187">An <strong data-start="42" data-end="61">embedded system</strong> is a <strong data-start="67" data-end="138">computer system designed to perform a specific task or set of tasks</strong> within a larger mechanical or electrical system.</p>
<p data-start="189" data-end="357">Unlike general-purpose computers (like laptops or smartphones), embedded systems are optimized for <strong data-start="288" data-end="318">specific control functions</strong>, often with <strong data-start="331" data-end="356">real-time constraints</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="359" data-end="362">
<h3 data-start="364" data-end="395">🔧 <strong data-start="371" data-end="395">Key Characteristics:</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="396" data-end="814">
<li data-start="396" data-end="508">
<p data-start="398" data-end="508"><strong data-start="398" data-end="420">Dedicated function</strong>: Designed for one purpose (e.g., controlling a washing machine, monitoring heart rate).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="509" data-end="578">
<p data-start="511" data-end="578"><strong data-start="511" data-end="535">Embedded in hardware</strong>: Integrated into the hardware it controls.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="579" data-end="665">
<p data-start="581" data-end="665"><strong data-start="581" data-end="604">Real-time operation</strong>: Often needs to respond to inputs within strict time limits.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="666" data-end="745">
<p data-start="668" data-end="745"><strong data-start="668" data-end="692">Resource-constrained</strong>: Limited memory, power, and processing capabilities.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="746" data-end="814">
<p data-start="748" data-end="814"><strong data-start="748" data-end="776">Reliability &amp; efficiency</strong>: Must work continuously and reliably.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="816" data-end="819">
<h3 data-start="821" data-end="861">📦 <strong data-start="828" data-end="861">Examples of Embedded Systems:</strong></h3>
<ul data-start="862" data-end="1067">
<li data-start="862" data-end="881">
<p data-start="864" data-end="881">Microwave ovens</p>
</li>
<li data-start="882" data-end="903">
<p data-start="884" data-end="903">Smart thermostats</p>
</li>
<li data-start="904" data-end="946">
<p data-start="906" data-end="946">Automotive engine control units (ECUs)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="947" data-end="980">
<p data-start="949" data-end="980">Industrial automation systems</p>
</li>
<li data-start="981" data-end="1031">
<p data-start="983" data-end="1031">IoT devices (like smart bulbs or door sensors)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1032" data-end="1067">
<p data-start="1034" data-end="1067">Medical devices (like pacemakers)</p>
</li>
</ul>";s:12:"availability";s:28:"{"op":"&","c":[],"showc":[]}";s:9:"component";N;s:6:"itemid";N;}i:12;O:8:"stdClass":7:{s:2:"id";s:2:"12";s:7:"section";s:1:"1";s:4:"name";s:30:"Programming in embedded system";s:7:"summary";s:1704:"<p data-start="206" data-end="457">This section focuses on the core programming principles used in embedded systems development. You’ll learn how to write efficient and reliable code for microcontrollers using the C programming language — the industry standard for embedded development.</p>
<p data-start="459" data-end="782">The module explores how low-level hardware control is achieved through code, including handling input/output (GPIO), managing memory, and working with timers and interrupts. You'll also understand the differences between general-purpose programming and embedded programming, especially in resource-constrained environments.</p>
<hr data-start="784" data-end="787">
<p data-start="789" data-end="808"><strong data-start="789" data-end="808">Topics Covered:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="809" data-end="1088">
<li data-start="809" data-end="845">
<p data-start="811" data-end="845">Basics of C for microcontrollers</p>
</li>
<li data-start="846" data-end="904">
<p data-start="848" data-end="904">Structure of embedded C programs (setup, loop, main())</p>
</li>
<li data-start="905" data-end="937">
<p data-start="907" data-end="937">Accessing hardware registers</p>
</li>
<li data-start="938" data-end="969">
<p data-start="940" data-end="969">Bit manipulation techniques</p>
</li>
<li data-start="970" data-end="1014">
<p data-start="972" data-end="1014">Polling vs. interrupt-driven programming</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1015" data-end="1053">
<p data-start="1017" data-end="1053">Using timers, delays, and counters</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1054" data-end="1088">
<p data-start="1056" data-end="1088">Writing and debugging firmware</p>
</li>
</ul>";s:12:"availability";s:28:"{"op":"&","c":[],"showc":[]}";s:9:"component";N;s:6:"itemid";N;}i:13;O:8:"stdClass":7:{s:2:"id";s:2:"13";s:7:"section";s:1:"2";s:4:"name";s:54:"Selection of Programming Language for Embedded Systems";s:7:"summary";s:2090:"<p data-start="225" data-end="494">Choosing the right programming language is a critical step in embedded systems development. This section introduces the key factors that influence language selection, such as system constraints, real-time requirements, hardware capabilities, and developer productivity.</p>
<p data-start="496" data-end="715">You will explore popular languages used in embedded development — including <strong data-start="572" data-end="577">C</strong>, <strong data-start="579" data-end="586">C++</strong>, <strong data-start="588" data-end="600">Assembly</strong>, and <strong data-start="606" data-end="645">Python (for high-level prototyping)</strong> — and understand their strengths, limitations, and typical use cases.</p>
<hr data-start="717" data-end="720">
<p data-start="722" data-end="741"><strong data-start="722" data-end="741">Topics Covered:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="742" data-end="1128">
<li data-start="742" data-end="822">
<p data-start="744" data-end="822">Criteria for selecting a language: performance, memory, portability, support</p>
</li>
<li data-start="823" data-end="878">
<p data-start="825" data-end="878">Why C is the industry standard for embedded systems</p>
</li>
<li data-start="879" data-end="941">
<p data-start="881" data-end="941">Role of Assembly in low-level programming and optimization</p>
</li>
<li data-start="942" data-end="997">
<p data-start="944" data-end="997">Use of C++ in object-oriented embedded applications</p>
</li>
<li data-start="998" data-end="1084">
<p data-start="1000" data-end="1084">When and where Python or MicroPython can be used (e.g., for rapid IoT prototyping)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1085" data-end="1128">
<p data-start="1087" data-end="1128">Real-world comparisons and case studies</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1130" data-end="1133">
<p data-start="1135" data-end="1321">By the end of this section, you'll understand how to make informed decisions when selecting a programming language based on project requirements, system resources, and development goals.</p>";s:12:"availability";s:28:"{"op":"&","c":[],"showc":[]}";s:9:"component";N;s:6:"itemid";N;}}s:9:"shortname";s:10:"Embedded C";s:8:"fullname";s:37:"Basics of Embedded system programming";s:6:"format";s:6:"topics";s:16:"enablecompletion";s:1:"1";s:9:"groupmode";s:1:"0";s:14:"groupmodeforce";s:1:"0";s:8:"cacherev";s:10:"1754328219";}s:7:"version";i:1754328219;}